// Growth of the specialized arrays must not corrupt the collector's heap
// accounting.
//
// Each of the three array kinds keeps its data in a separate realloc'd block,
// and the collector subtracts the *current* size when it frees one. If the
// growth is not recorded, every growth drifts heap_bytes downward until it
// wraps negative -- and then every allocation trips the collection threshold.
// The visible result is a collector running a few times per push: a 27x-170x
// slowdown on any append-heavy loop, measured as a rag retrieval run at 253
// seconds instead of 0.6.
//
// The cycle count is the tripwire, asserted here rather than in a golden file:
// the two backends allocate different totals, so the count differs between
// them, but a broken accounting makes it explode by four orders of magnitude
// on either one. The threshold is 1000; a healthy run is in single digits.
i = 0
xs = []
ys = []
zs = []
while i < 200000 {
  xs.push(i)
  ys.push(i * 1.5)
  zs.push("row " + str(i))
  i = i + 1
}
print("lens", len(xs), len(ys), len(zs))
print("sum", sum(xs) % 1000000)

stats = gc("stats")
cycles = 0
for field in stats.split(" ") {
  if field.startswith("cycles=") {
    // slice(a, b) is inclusive of b and clamps past the end
    cycles = int(field.slice(7, len(field)))
  }
}
// The count itself differs between backends (they allocate different totals),
// so the golden file carries the assertion rather than the number. A broken
// accounting puts it in the tens of thousands.
print("accounting", cycles < 1000)
